Different providers often require different query syntaxes. OpenAlex works well
with natural language queries like \enquote{llm programming education}, while
Web of Science requires Boolean syntax like \enquote{llm AND programming AND
education}. When building a review session across multiple providers, we need to
track which query was used with which provider so that reports accurately
reflect the search methodology.

We provide helper methods for managing these records. The
[[add_query_provider_pair]] method records a search only if no record
with its key (query, provider and filters, \cref{sec:search-record-key})
is present yet, preventing duplicates when
appending results to an existing session; its [[filters]] and [[limit]]
arguments default to [[None]] so older callers that only know the query and
provider keep working. The [[query_provider_pairs]] property derives the
[[(query, provider)]] pairs from the records, so every reader written against
the old field is unaffected; a pair may now occur twice, once per set of
filters, as it is two searches. The [[queries_for_provider]] method retrieves all
queries used with a specific provider, and [[search_record_for]] retrieves the
first whole record for a provider---query, filters, and limit.
[[search_record]] looks a record up by its full key, which is what a
paper's [[found_by]] link holds and what [[found-via]] needs to rebuild the
search.  [[search_record_for]] cannot serve that purpose: a session with
several queries on one provider has several records for it, and the first
is merely the most recent search
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/128}).  The same holds one
level down for an old link that names no filters (a filters key of
[[None]]): [[search_record]] answers it only when exactly one record has
that query and provider.  With two, the link could have come from
either, and picking one would name filters that may never have returned
the paper---the mistake of \#128 again---so it answers [[None]] and the
caller reports the link as unresolved.

<<query-provider pair methods>>=
def add_query_provider_pair(
    self,
    query: str,
    provider: str,
    filters: dict | None = None,
    limit: int | None = None,
    *,
    hits: int | None = None,
    error: str | None = None,
    retries: int = 0,
    via: str | None = None,
) -> None:
    """
    Record a search unless a record with its key is already present.

    The key is the query, the provider and the filters (see
    `SearchRecord.key`), so the same query with other filters is
    recorded as a search of its own.

    This tracks which query was used with which provider, enabling
    different queries for different providers (e.g., natural language
    for OpenAlex, Boolean syntax for Web of Science), together with the
    filters and limit applied so the search can be reproduced exactly,
    and its outcome (hits, error, retries).
    """
    record = SearchRecord(
        query, provider, filters, limit,
        hits=hits, error=error, retries=retries, via=via,
    )
    if record.key not in {r.key for r in self.search_records}:
        self.search_records.append(record)

@property
def query_provider_pairs(self) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """The ``(query, provider)`` pairs, derived from the search records."""
    return [(r.query, r.provider) for r in self.search_records]

def queries_for_provider(self, provider: str) -> list[str]:
    """
    Get all queries used with a specific provider.

    Returns a list of queries that were used to search the given provider.
    """
    return [r.query for r in self.search_records if r.provider == provider]

def search_record_for(self, provider: str) -> SearchRecord | None:
    """
    Get the first recorded search for a provider, or ``None``.

    Returns the whole record---query, filters, and limit---so callers can
    reproduce the exact search that surfaced a paper from that provider.
    """
    for record in self.search_records:
        if record.provider == provider:
            return record
    return None

def search_record(
    self, query: str, provider: str, key: str | None = None
) -> SearchRecord | None:
    """Get the record of the search `(query, provider, key)`, or `None`.

    `key` is the `filters_key` of the search's filters. With `key`
    `None` (an old link that names no filters) the record is returned
    only if it is the one record with that query and provider.
    """
    if key is not None:
        for record in self.search_records:
            if record.key == (query, provider, key):
                return record
        return None
    same_pair = [
        record for record in self.search_records
        if (record.query, record.provider) == (query, provider)
    ]
    return same_pair[0] if len(same_pair) == 1 else None
