\label{sec:legacy-records}
A legacy record (\cref{sec:search-record-key}) is one search of its
query and provider with unknown filters.  When this run searched the
same query on the same provider, we do what was done before filters
joined the key: the new record replaces it, whatever its filters
(unless the new run failed and the legacy one answered, which the
rule above already settled the old way).  The comparison is with this
run's own records only ([[current_run]]); a record that an earlier
session added is no evidence about the legacy search.

The papers of the replaced search keep their links, which now point at
the record that replaced it.  That is what the session said before
filters joined the key: its one record of the pair stood for every run
of it.  [[replaced_links]] maps each replaced key to its successor, and
once all decisions are built every link is moved
([[<<point links at the records that replaced theirs>>]]); duplicates
that the move creates are merged, keeping the first.

<<let a re-run replace a legacy record>>=
if record.is_legacy:
    successor = next(
        (r for r in current_run
         if (r.query, r.provider) == (record.query, record.provider)),
        None,
    )
    if successor is not None:
        kept = any(r is record for r in session.search_records)
        if not kept and successor.key != record.key:
            replaced_links[record.key] = successor.key
        continue
<<point links at the records that replaced theirs>>=
if replaced_links:
    for decision in session.decisions:
        decision.found_by = without_superseded_links(list(dict.fromkeys(
            replaced_links.get(link, link) for link in decision.found_by
        )))
