\subsubsection{Finding the sessions that hold a paper}
\label{sec:review-sessions-mentioning}

[[prov found-via]] needs every session that holds one paper.  Loading all
sessions to look took over a minute on a data directory of 117 sessions
(300\,MB): [[list_sessions]] decodes every file for its counts, loading
decodes it again, and loading also builds a [[Paper]] for every decision.
JSON decoding and building the papers are the cost, reading the bytes is
not, so [[sessions_mentioning]] reads each file as text and decodes only
the files in which the paper's id occurs.

That shortcut is sound because of what the file holds.  Every decision
stores its [[paper_id]] (and every session format has), so a file that
holds the paper contains its id as text.  Three details keep the test from
missing a file.  It ignores case, since stored DOIs and the id a user
types may differ in case.  It drops a [[doi:]] prefix, so the paper's own
[[doi]] field matches too.  And for an arXiv DOI it also looks for the
bare arXiv id: loading derives that DOI for papers stored without one
([[_normalize_paper_identifiers]]), and saving does not write it back, so
the file may hold only the arXiv URL.  A fragment with a quote, a
backslash or a non-ASCII character could be escaped in the JSON text, so
then we skip the shortcut and decode every file: slower, never wrong.
Because the needles are ASCII, the test can run on the file's bytes:
[[bytes.lower]] folds only ASCII letters, which are the only ones a needle
has, and it skips the decoding and the Unicode case mapping that made a
[[str]] version take four of its six seconds.

The caller still matches each loaded decision against the id; the text
test only decides which files are worth decoding.  Files that cannot be
read or parsed are reported with a warning, as [[list_sessions]] does, but
only when they mention the paper, since only then could they hide its
provenance.

<<review session functions>>=
def sessions_mentioning(fragment: str) -> list[ReviewSession]:
    """Load the saved sessions whose file mentions `fragment`, newest first.

    `fragment` is a paper id or a part of one (a bare DOI, say). Only
    files whose text contains it are parsed, which makes the lookup cheap
    on a large data directory. The caller still has to match the loaded
    decisions against `fragment`.
    """
    needles = _mention_needles(fragment)
    sessions = []
    for session_file in _get_sessions_dir().glob("*.json"):
        if needles is not None:
            try:
                text = session_file.read_bytes().lower()
            except OSError as error:
                logger.warning(
                    "Cannot read session file %s: %s", session_file, error
                )
                continue
            if not any(needle in text for needle in needles):
                continue
        try:
            sessions.append(read_session_file(session_file))
        except SessionFileError as error:
            logger.warning("%s", error)
    return sorted(sessions, key=lambda s: s.timestamp, reverse=True)


def _mention_needles(fragment: str) -> set[bytes] | None:
    """The lower-case texts a session file holding `fragment` contains.

    None when `fragment` could be escaped in JSON text, so that every
    file must be parsed.
    """
    text = fragment.lower().removeprefix("doi:")
    if not text.isascii() or '"' in text or "\\" in text:
        return None
    needles = {text}
    if "arxiv." in text:
        needles.add(text.split("arxiv.", 1)[1])
    return {needle.encode("ascii") for needle in needles}
@

We check the three claims the shortcut rests on.  A broken file that does
not mention the paper is never decoded, so it raises no warning, while one
that does is still reported.  An arXiv paper saved without a DOI,
which gets one only when loaded, is found by that DOI.  And an id that
the file stores escaped, as files written with [[ensure_ascii]] do, is
still found.

