class TestReportModes:
    def _session(self):
        session = ReviewSession(
            query="q", providers=["p"], timestamp=datetime.now(),
        )
        session.decisions = [
            ReviewDecision(
                paper=Paper(title="P", authors=["A"], year=2024),
                provider="p", status=DecisionStatus.KEPT, tags=["ml"],
            ),
        ]
        return session

    def test_default_is_standalone(self, tmp_path):
        path = tmp_path / "report.tex"
        generate_latex_report(self._session(), path)
        content = path.read_text()
        assert content.startswith(r"\documentclass{article}")
        assert r"\addbibresource{report.bib}" in content
        assert r"\begin{refsection}" not in content
        assert content.rstrip().endswith(r"\end{document}")

    def test_fragment_omits_skeleton_and_writes_bib(self, tmp_path):
        path = tmp_path / "report.tex"
        generate_latex_report(self._session(), path, standalone=False)
        content = path.read_text()
        assert r"\documentclass" not in content
        assert r"\begin{document}" not in content
        assert "\n\\addbibresource" not in content
        assert "report.bib" in content
        assert r"\begin{refsection}" in content
        assert r"\section{Kept Papers}" in content
        assert (tmp_path / "report.bib").read_text().startswith("@article{")
@

\subsection{Audit table}
\label{sec:audit-table}

A backing appendix in a paper must let a reader without access to the
session see every candidate the search returned and why each one was
kept or excluded. The review report is too long for that and is a
document of its own; what the appendix needs is one table. Its rows are
papers, its last column is the reason, and it is always a fragment
because it is meant to be [[\input]] next to the claim it backs.

The reason column encodes the review's own vocabulary. A paper a human
kept is \emph{cited}, under the theme its tags name; the caller may map
theme tags to readable names. The remaining papers were classified, by a
human or by [[scholar llm classify]], into one of four categories that a
two-sided literature search produces: the paper supports the claim,
qualifies or contradicts it, belongs to an adjacent subtopic, or is a
false hit from another field. The order of the categories doubles as the
sort order, so the table reads from the strongest evidence downwards.
Papers still pending sort after them, and last come the rows that are
only bookkeeping: a review also tags records it deliberately does not
cite---a duplicate record, a reprint, the thesis behind a cited paper,
a record judged on its title alone---and those tags are \emph{notes},
not themes. Treating them as themes once inflated a cited count from
thirteen to seventeen.

The caption never names the session: session names are the author's
bookkeeping and must not appear in compiled text. The fragment carries
the name in a comment instead, and the [[--track]] text identifies the
table for the reader.

