\paragraph{Semantic searches in the audit table.}
A session can also hold semantic searches ([[openalex-semantic]],
\cref{sec:ranked-list}), and they are not database searches.  Each
returned a ranked list of a fixed length, not every record that
matched, so they cover nothing in the sense the \enquote{Databases:}
sentence claims coverage.  Naming them there would let the appendix
claim that a database was searched which was only sampled.  The
sentence therefore skips them, both as records and as provider codes in
the rows, and so does the count of failures behind its notes: a failed
semantic check says nothing about whether the database searches
covered their queries.

They are not hidden either.  Their records still appear among the
rows, marked [[OA-sem]] in the Provider column, and the caption gets a
sentence of its own that says where those rows came from and what
kind of search it was: a check on the keyword searches, of the stated
length, with no count of matches.  The length comes from the records
(the limit asked for, else the hits).  When every semantic search
asked for the same number, the sentence names it (\enquote{the 50
works}); when the questions asked for different numbers, or some
records do not say, it gives the largest as a bound (\enquote{up to 50
works}), since naming the largest as every question's length would
overstate the shorter ones.  A session whose records give no length at
all gets the sentence without a number rather than a guessed one.

<<review report generation>>=
RANKED_SHORT_NAMES = frozenset(
    PROVIDER_SHORT_NAMES[name] for name in RANKED_PROVIDERS
)


def audit_semantic_sentence(
    rows: list[AuditRow],
    strings: dict,
    records: list[SearchRecord] | None = None,
) -> str:
    """Say which rows came from a semantic search, or "" if none did."""
    ranked = [r for r in records or [] if is_ranked_provider(r.provider)]
    in_rows = any(
        provider in RANKED_SHORT_NAMES
        for row in rows for provider in row.providers
    )
    if not ranked and not in_rows:
        return ""
    lengths = {r.limit or r.hits or None for r in ranked}
    known = {n for n in lengths if n}
    if not known:
        top = strings["semantic_top_unknown"]
    elif len(lengths) == 1:
        top = strings["semantic_top"].format(n=known.pop())
    else:
        top = strings["semantic_top_max"].format(n=max(known))
    return strings["semantic"].format(top=top)
